speechtext:
I am in the hands of the committee, but I wonder whether it would suit the convenience of hon. members if I
were to make a brief explanatory statement on these various resolutions as they are called, and then other hon. members could offer their comments. If that meets with the wishes of the committee I might proceed in that way.
The first and second resolutions taken together give effect to the revision and simplification of the personal income tax structure, which is proposed for 1947. The single schedule of graduated rates set forth in resolution 1; the exemption of $750 for a single person and $1,500 for a married person, and the further exemptions for dependents set forth in resolution 2, replace the entire present personal income tax rate structure, which includes the following;
1.	The normal tax of 7 per cent, which in the case of certain taxpayers is increased to 8 per cent at $1,800 and to 9 per cent at $3,000;
2.	The tax credit of $28 allowed against normal tax for each dependent;
3.	The schedule of graduated rates in the present law;
4.	The $660 deduction from income, the $150 tax credit allowed for a wife and the $80 tax credit allowed for a child under the graduated tax;
5.	The formula by which the savings portion of the total tax is calculated. While the refundable tax has not been imposed since 1944, the tax liability under the present law is still determined by calculating the total tax and deducting therefrom the savings portion to arrive at the actual balance of tax payable.
6.	The family allowance recovery tables which were introduced as a temporary supplement to the present tax structure, to give effect to the principle approved by the house that the taxpayer should not benefit in full from both family allowance payments and relief for the same child granted under the income tax. As the committee will appreciate, there is a very real simplification of the personal income tax structure. The substantial benefits to the taxpayer arising from this over-all revision were fully explained in the budget speech, and have since been set forth in the house.
I wish again briefly to summarize those advantages:
1.	Between 550,000 and 600,000 taxpayers will be completely exempt from taxation in 1947.
2.	Including adjustments of family allowances, the total benefit to the taxpayers in the proposal will be $155 million, and relief
Income War Tax

amounting to 25 per cent of our present income tax revenues, coming on top of the 16 per cent already allowed for 1946.
3.	Of the total relief of S155 million, about 70 per cent will accrue to taxpayers with incomes under $3,000; 83 per cent will accrue to taxpayers with incomes under $5,000 and 90 per cent will accrue to taxpayers with incomes under $10,000.
4.	Our exemptions under the proposal will be higher than those of any other Englishspeaking country in the world.
